About Topica
Version @VERSION@

Topica is a Markdown writer for blogs made with Jekyll, Hugo and other static site generators.

Writing a post
- File > New Post starts from front matter: the lines between the two "---" at the top. To start from your own, put a text file called Topica Template beside Topica or with its preferences; {{date}} in it becomes today's date. On System 7, any post made a stationery pad (the Finder's Get Info) opens the same way, as a new untitled post.
- On System 7.5 and later, drag selected text to move it within a post, or to copy it to another window or the desktop.
- Post > Post Info (Command-E) fills in the title, description, category, tags, layout and date, in YAML front matter (between --- lines) or Hugo's TOML (between +++ lines).
- Post > Insert Image writes a placeholder for a picture you'll add to the blog later.
- File > Save suggests a name made of today's date and the title, like 2026-10-22-my-post.md.

Formatting
Topica shows Markdown's formatting as you write: headings and strong text in bold, emphasis in italics, code in Monaco, link text underlined. Post > Hide Formatting shows plain text. On a 68000 Mac (the Macintosh Plus, SE, 512Ke) formatting starts off, because it makes long posts slow to open; Post > Show Formatting turns it on.

Post > Theme colors the post, its status line and the preview: Light or Dark on any Mac, and on a color Mac Solarized, TokyoNight, Amber CRT, System 7, Dracula, Nord, Green Screen, Classic, Monokai and Gruvbox. Printing stays black on white. Topica Lite has Light and Dark.

The Headings menu lists the post's headings. Choose one to go there.

Post > Insert Link (Command-K) puts a link around the selected text.

Preview
Post > Show Preview (Command-R) shows the post laid out like its web page, with the title and date from the front matter, its pictures and the links listed at the end. On a big enough screen it opens beside the post, in the same window; drag the line between them to change their widths. On a Macintosh Plus or SE it's a window of its own. It follows the post you're writing and catches up when you pause. Double-click in the preview to go to that place in the post.
Pictures show when the blog's files are on the Mac: Topica looks for an image's address from the post's folder, and for an address from the site's top (/assets/...) in the folders above it. QuickTime 2.5 or later draws JPEG, GIF and PNG; without it, PICT files show. A picture Topica can't find or draw shows as a box.

Printing and HTML
File > Print (Command-P) prints the post as the preview shows it. File > Export HTML saves it as a web page you can open in a browser.

Spelling
Edit > Check Spelling (Command-;) goes through the post from the caret and stops at each word it doesn't know, with suggestions. Learn keeps a word in Topica Words, in the Preferences folder (System 7) or the System Folder. The dictionary is the file Topica Dictionary, in the same folder as Topica; it is made from SCOWL, by Kevin Atkinson and others (see Dictionary Licence). On System 7, Edit > Check Spelling With hands the post to a Word Services spelling checker such as Excalibur instead; one Undo takes back all of its corrections.

vi keys
Edit > Preferences can turn on vi keys: normal, insert and visual modes, as in vi. Escape is the Esc key, or Command-period on keyboards without one; "jk leaves insert mode" makes typing j then k quickly do it too. The status line shows INSERT and VISUAL.

Publishing
Post > Publish sends the post to topica-bridge, a small server on your network, which puts it on your blog's drafts branch; you merge that branch when you want the post on the site. The dialog suggests where the post goes (_posts with its date, _drafts for a draft, or content/posts for Hugo) and remembers the bridge and its key. The bridge is an address or a name with its port, like 192.0.2.5:8023 or bridge.example.com:8023. If your blog keeps posts in a folder for each year, publish one as _posts/2026/2026-10-23-title.md and Topica suggests that form from then on.
Publishing goes on in the background, so you can go on writing, or close the post: it sends the post as it was when you chose Publish. The post's status line says how far it has got, and Post > Publish becomes Stop Publishing until it's done. The result comes in a note; if you're in another program, the Mac tells you first. Topica needs MacTCP for this, or the talkip build of Topica Lite on a Mac with LocalTalk and a MacIP gateway.

Undo and Redo
Command-Z undoes, many steps back. Command-Shift-Z redoes.

Find
Edit > Find (Command-F) finds and replaces. Command-G finds again; Command-= replaces the selection and finds again.

Preferences
Edit > Preferences sets the font, Jekyll or Hugo, how posts are saved, the image folder and how often Topica keeps a recovery copy. They are kept in Topica Preferences, a text file in the Preferences folder (System 7) or the System Folder.

After a crash
While you write, Topica keeps a copy of each unsaved post in the System Folder's Topica Recovery folder. If the Mac crashes, Topica opens those posts again the next time it starts.

Other characters
Posts are saved in UTF-8, the way a blog wants them. Characters the Macintosh can't show, such as emoji, appear as codes like &#x1F600; while you write and go back to the real character when you save.

Limits
Topica opens posts of up to 32,000 characters. Topica Lite, for 512K Macs, opens two windows at a time. The rest of the limits, and news of Topica, are at github.com/ecliptik/topica.

